vulnerabilities that failed after the announcement.\n- The settings file uTalk.json now lives in userConfig\\ChaiChaimee\\uTalk\\. If an old copy exists in userConfig or userConfig\\ChaiChaimee, it is moved there at startup and the old file is deleted once the new one is checked.\n- NVDA+Alt+T now takes up to four taps: swap languages, open settings, turn uTalk off or on for the current application, mute or unmute. It is listed again in Input Gestures as \"uTalk multi tap\". The mute and current application commands are also listed there with no key assigned.\n- Copy and Cut are announced only when the clipboard really changed. Otherwise uTalk says \"Nothing to copy\" or \"Nothing to cut\". Paste is not announced in browse mode or in read-only fields. This can be turned off in settings.\n- Holding or repeating a shortcut now gives one announcement instead of a queue of them.\n- uTalk is silent in Command Prompt, PowerShell and Windows Terminal by default. Per-application rules (name=off, name=main or name=alt) can be edited in settings.\n- New output choices: speech and braille, speech only, braille only, or beep only.\n- New detail level: copy, cut and paste report the number of characters and lines, for example \"copy 2 lines 50 characters\". A line break at the end of the text is not counted as an extra line. The comma between the parts was removed. In editable fields, undo and redo also say what was removed or added.\n- If no alternate language is set, the language toggle says so instead of going silent. A command with an empty alternate phrase now uses its English phrase.\n- The settings dialog refuses text longer than 200 characters and application rules that are not valid, and tells you which field to fix.\n- Wrong value types in uTalk.json are replaced by the defaults. The file is only written when something changed.\n- Fixed an error when saving settings if the temporary file could not be created. The settings dump in the log moved to debug level.\n- Code n